{
  "$comment": "The format-aware document property support registry (Phase 13 §61, §62). One table, consumed by the Python analyzers, the API and the web UI, so PDF compatibility is never described in three places that can disagree. EXACT means the format stores the property and PalCert reads it. DERIVED means PalCert computes it from the format under a documented definition, and evidence is labelled as derived. UNSUPPORTED means the format cannot answer it and a rule about it is INDETERMINATE rather than PASS. The registry deliberately does NOT merge a similar measurement with a different meaning: a DOCX marginLeft is an authoring instruction and a PDF observedContentInsetLeft is a measurement of where ink happens to start, so they are separate rows and a margin rule is not silently satisfied by an inset. Version 2 adds an optional `elementTypes` to a row: the element types an ELEMENT-scoped property is actually emitted for. Absent means the row does not say, which is how every Phase 13 row was written and stays written. Present, it is a closed list, and a rule naming an element type outside it is asking for a fact no analyzer produces — which matters for the Phase 18 citation properties, because the neighbouring REFERENCE_ENTRY element type carries no properties at all and an undeclared ELEMENT row would read as though it did. Version 3 adds ADVISORY, which is not a weaker EXACT and not a variant of DERIVED. DERIVED is a deterministic computation over what the format does store — an ink inset is measured, exhaustively and repeatably. ADVISORY is a recognition: PalCert believes it found something, the population is a lower bound rather than a total, and the evidence is labelled HEURISTIC all the way to the verdict. A rule about an ADVISORY property is INDETERMINATE with evidence level ADVISORY, never PASS and never FAIL, so the support level and the evaluator agree on what the number is worth.",
  "registryVersion": 3,
  "supportLevels": ["EXACT", "DERIVED", "ADVISORY", "UNSUPPORTED"],
  "formats": ["DOCX", "PDF"],
  "distinctSemantics": [
    {
      "$comment": "Pairs that measure something similar and mean something different. Never merge them.",
      "docx": "marginLeft",
      "pdf": "observedContentInsetLeft"
    },
    {
      "docx": "marginRight",
      "pdf": "observedContentInsetRight"
    },
    {
      "docx": "marginTop",
